If you're using Laravel 10, you shouldn't hardcode the pagination HTML. Instead, use Laravel's built-in pagination.

Controller

Replace get() with paginate():

$cars = Car::with([
        'brand',
        'model',
        'thumbnail',
        'images'
    ])
    ->where('status', 1)
    ->orderBy('id', 'desc')
    ->paginate(12); // 12 cars per page

If you already have filters:

$cars = Car::with([
        'brand',
        'model',
        'thumbnail'
    ])
    ->where('status', 1)
    ->paginate(12)
    ->withQueryString(); // Keeps existing query parameters
Blade

Replace your static HTML with:

@if($cars->hasPages())
<div class="col-md-12 col-xs-12 col-sm-12 no-padding">
    <div class="rating-pagination pagination-lg">
        {{ $cars->withQueryString()->links() }}
    </div>
</div>
@endif
If you want the pagination to look exactly like your HTML

Publish Laravel pagination views:

php artisan vendor:publish --tag=laravel-pagination

It will create:

resources/views/vendor/pagination/

Copy bootstrap-4.blade.php (or bootstrap-5.blade.php) and rename it:

inventory-pagination.blade.php

Then customize it like this:

@if ($paginator->hasPages())
<nav>
    @if ($paginator->onFirstPage())
        <span class="page-numbers disabled">« Previous</span>
    @else
        <a class="prev page-numbers" href="{{ $paginator->previousPageUrl() }}">
            « Previous Page
        </a>
    @endif

    @foreach ($elements as $element)

        @if (is_string($element))
            <span class="page-numbers dots">{{ $element }}</span>
        @endif

        @if (is_array($element))
            @foreach ($element as $page => $url)

                @if ($page == $paginator->currentPage())
                    <span class="page-numbers current">{{ $page }}</span>
                @else
                    <a class="page-numbers" href="{{ $url }}">{{ $page }}</a>
                @endif

            @endforeach
        @endif

    @endforeach

    @if ($paginator->hasMorePages())
        <a class="next page-numbers" href="{{ $paginator->nextPageUrl() }}">
            Next Page »
        </a>
    @else
        <span class="page-numbers disabled">Next Page »</span>
    @endif
</nav>
@endif

Display it using:

<div class="rating-pagination pagination-lg">
    {{ $cars->withQueryString()->links('vendor.pagination.inventory-pagination') }}
</div>
CSS

To match your theme:

.rating-pagination {
    text-align: center;
    margin: 30px 0;
}

.rating-pagination .page-numbers {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 10px 16px;
    margin: 0 4px;
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    color: #333;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.rating-pagination .page-numbers.current {
    background: #ffcc00;
    color: #000;
    border-color: #ffcc00;
}

.rating-pagination .page-numbers:hover {
    background: #f5f5f5;
}

.rating-pagination .page-numbers.dots {
    border: none;
    background: transparent;
}

.rating-pagination .page-numbers.disabled {
    opacity: .5;
    pointer-events: none;
}

This will produce pagination very similar to:

1 2 3 ... 8 Next Page »

while automatically handling page numbers, ellipses (...), previous/next links, and preserving query string parameters.
